cause with a higher container value the routine drew more.\n\nAlternatively, maybe 0x066F is the number of hearts CONTAINERS minus one, and 0x0670 is current health. At clean 0x066F=34, 0x0670=255. 34 = 0x22... no.\n\nI'm going in circles. Let me do a decisive experiment using trace_writes to see who reads/writes 0x066F, and look at disassembly. Actually, the best: use the game itself. Let me find the *current health* by a clean differential: from clean state, take exactly one hit and read which bytes changed among a targeted range. But movement noise.\n\nActually, here's a cleaner idea: Use `watch` on a set of candidate addresses during a damage event. But I can also just take a hit with minimal movement by going to the enemy screen and standing.\n\nAlternatively — use the earlier diff intersection (d1 ∩ d2 = 29 addresses). In that list, 0x061D old 12→1, 0x066F 33→32, 0x0670 254→0. Since death resets health to 0 on the Continue screen, the health byte should have gone to 0. 0x061D went 12→1 (not 0). 0x066F went 33→32 (not 0). 0x0670 went 254→0! So maybe 0x0670 is current health and at clean it's 255 (or the display derives differently)? Hmm, 254→0 at death. And at clean 0x0670=255. Hmm. 255 = full? 254 = after something? Then death = 0.\n\nBut writing 0x066F changed the hearts display, while 0x0670 might be health.\n\nHmm, wait: maybe 0x066F = heart containers (max), and 0x0670 = current health. At clean: containers=34? no, that would display 34 hearts.\n\nI think my whole approach of interpreting is flawed. Let me look at actual Zelda RAM knowledge... but \"without any reference\" - I shouldn't look it up. But I can reason from the game.\n\nLet me get precise: read the HUD heart row tiles in the clean state, then in a state with one hit, to see exactly what changes. The tiles themselves won't encode RAM but will tell me how many full/empty.\n\nActually, let me step back and use the sanctioned differential method properly, on hearts, with in-place damage. That give