{"type":"thinking","text":"0x22 (34) → 3 red hearts (3 full hearts, no pale). So:\n- 0x10 → 1 full + 2 empty\n- 0x22 → 3 full (looks identical to clean)\n- 0x30 → earlier 3 full + 3 empty (6 slots)?? \n\nWait, maybe 0x22 with 3 full is because health >= 3 hearts. And the pale/empty count: 0x10 gave exactly 3 slots (1 full + 2 pale). But 0x30 gave 6? Let me retest 0x30 now.\n\nHmm, maybe I mis-saw earlier. Let me retest 0x30.\n\nActually, maybe the number of slots = number of containers stored elsewhere = 3, and what I saw as \"3 pale\" after writing 0x30 were actually 3 red + 3 pale because the value 0x30 (48) overflowed and drew extra. Let me just retest and look carefully.\n\nActually maybe the value isn't 0x10 per heart. Let's map:\n- 0x10 = 16 → 1 full (of 3)\n- 0x22 = 34 → 3 full\nThat's not consistent with 16 per heart (34 would be 2.125). Unless the threshold for the 3rd heart is 0x22? Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the display is: full hearts = (value >> 4) + something? 16>>4=1 → 1 full. 34>>4=2 → 2 full, but display showed 3. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the health is stored as half-hearts: each heart = 2 units, value 34 → 17 hearts? no.\n\nHold on. Maybe 0x066F is NOT health. Maybe it's the \"heart container\" count, and the display shows `containers` slots, with fullness determined by a different variable (0x0670?). Let's check:\n- Clean: 0x066F=34, 0x0670=255. Display 3 full hearts.\n- 0x066F=16, 0x0670=255. Display: 1 full + 2 empty.\n\nHmm, if 0x066F were containers, changing it would change the number of slots, not fill. But display kept 3 slots and changed fill. So 0x066F = fullness. Good.\n\n- 0x066F=34 → 3 full (display 3 red)\n- 0x066F=16 → 1 full + 2 empty\n\nSo threshold: full hearts = ceil? or floor? value 16 → 1; 34 → 3. If each heart = 16 units: 16→1, 34→2.125→2 full + maybe a partial drawn as tiny (looks empty?). Hmm, but we saw 3 full red at 34. Let me look again at the 0x22 screenshot: 3 red hearts. Yes.\n\nAlternatively each heart = 12 units: 34/12=2.83→2 full + partial; 16/12=1.33→1 full. Hmm.\n\nMaybe the display fills hearts based on value compared to thresholds stored in a table (e.g., heart i full if value >= some). \n\nActually, maybe the encoding is: value = (full_hearts << 4) | (partial in 1/16)? Then 34 = 0x22 → 2 full + 2/16 partial. Display would show 2 full + a tiny partial. But we saw 3 red. Unless the third \"red heart\" is actually 2/16 partial rendered... no, a partial heart is drawn as a full-outline with partial fill; at 2/16 it'd be nearly empty.\n\nHmm, I'm clearly misreading the tiny hearts. Let me look at the 0x10 vs 0x22 screenshots programmatically: read the nametable heart row and/or get_pixel_color at heart positions to count filled pixels.\n\nActually, better: the HUD hearts are drawn as sprites? No, get_tile_at_pixel said background tile 36. The difference between full and empty is likely the TILE (two different heart tiles). Let me read the nametable heart row for 0x10 and 0x22 and compare tile IDs. That'll tell me exactly how many full hearts and total slots.\n\nLet me read nametable rows 5-7 cols 24-31 now (0x22 state).\n\nEarlier for 0x30 state I read and got row6 cols 24-31: [102,102,36,36,36,36,36,36]. Hmm that was 0x30 state? Actually the read was after 0x30 write. It showed 102 at cols 24,25 and 36 at 26-31. Wait, that means the heart tiles are at cols 26-31 (6 tiles of 36) and cols 24-25 = 102. Hmm, but 6 tiles of the SAME tile 36 → all 6 look identical. But the screenshot showed 3 red + 3 pale. So full vs empty must differ by PALETTE, not tile. But the whole row is palette 1...\n\nHmm, unless the full hearts use tile 36 and empty use tile 36 too but... no.\n\nWait, maybe the heart row is actually drawn at a different y. Let me get exact: get_tile_at_pixel(216,50) gave tile 36, palette 1, tile_x=27, tile_y=6. So (27,6) is a heart. In the nametable read of the 0x30 state, (27,6)=36. Hmm all of 26-31 are 36.\n\nSo in the 0x30 state, the 6 heart positions were all tile 36. But