art would set it to... The game would need to represent half hearts. Our data says it can't. Unless the game stores health in low nibble as HALF-hearts but the display tile 66 is a half-heart that looks like a full pale heart! Wait, we determined tile 66 is solid peach. In Zelda, empty hearts are... let me think: actually in Zelda, an empty heart container is drawn as a white/pale heart OUTLINE (unfilled). A half heart is drawn as half red, half outline. But maybe in this ROM's CHR, the \"empty\" heart is solid pale. Hmm.\n\nHmm, if tile 66 were a HALF heart (drawn as... no, half hearts aren't solid pale).\n\nLet me settle it: at value 0x10 (1 heart), tiles = F2,66. If 66 is a half heart, the display shows 1.5 hearts. If 66 is empty, it shows 1.0 heart. The pixel is solid peach. In Zelda's HUD, the empty heart is drawn as a heart SHAPE in a pale color (actually yes! In Zelda 1 the empty heart containers appear as pale/white hearts, not outlines). And half hearts appear as a heart with half red/half pale. So a solid pale heart = empty. So 0x10 = 1 heart full, 1 empty, and NO third slot → 2 containers? But real containers = 3.\n\nUnless... the game only draws as many slots as there are \"hearts to account for\": Hmm.\n\nOK, you know what, let me just accept the empirical encoding and also report the container aspect: the high nibble defines how many slots are drawn. I'll describe it as: 0x066F holds hearts: high nibble = (max hearts - 1), low nibble = (current full hearts - 1). Hmm, but I want to double-check with real gameplay: at clean, 0x22 → 3 containers, 3 full ✓. After a hit, 0x21 → 3 containers, 2 full. But the actual game after a hit shows 2.5 hearts! Let me verify what the real display showed after a hit. Earlier screenshot (7f859f) after damage: I described \"2.5 hearts\". Let me re-examine: the HUD showed \"❤❤\" and a partial. Hmm. If the display were 2 full + 1 empty (pale), I'd describe 3 shapes. Let me recheck by reading the nametable at that moment... can't, it's past.\n\nHmm, let me just take damage in a controlled way again and read the nametable. But that takes time.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the true health variable is elsewhere and 0x066F is just the \"full hearts\" count and the number of slots isn't containers but something else. But our writes to 0x066F clearly changed the slot count, so the display reads 0x066F for slots.\n\nHmm hold on, maybe the slot count in the display is NOT from 0x066F but from the \"empty\" tiles remaining from a previous draw. I.e., when we write a lower value, the routine draws new hearts but doesn't erase the extra ones, so blanks appear... Actually when I wrote 0x10, we got F2,66,blank. If the previous state had 3 tiles drawn (F2,F2,66 from 0x21), then writing 0x10 should show F2,66,66 (if it redraws 2 slots and leaves the third) — but we got blank at slot 3. Hmm, unless the routine redrew all 3 as F2,66,blank.\n\nAnd when I wrote 0x30, slots became 4 (F2,66,66,66). So the display drew 4 tiles. So the routine definitely uses 0x066F's high nibble to decide the count.\n\nHmm, unless the count comes from... hmm, what if the game draws \"value & 0x0F\" full hearts + 1 empty heart? For 0x22: low=2 → 2 full + 1 empty = 3 slots. But we saw 3 FULL (F2,F2,F2). Hmm no.\n\nWhat if it draws (value >> 4) + (value & 0x0F)? no.\n\nLet me just do a couple more writes to be sure about \"full = low+1\" vs \"full = low\": \n- 0x066F = 0x20 (32): model → containers 3, full = low+1 = 1 → F2,66,66. \n- 0x066F = 0x0F (15): containers 1, full capped 1 → F2.\nLet me test 0x20. If it shows 1 full + 2 empty, model confirmed (full=low+1). If it shows 0 full + 3 empty (all 66), then full=low.\n\nLet me test 0x20.\n\nActually earlier at the death screen 0x066F was 0x20, and the Continue screen didn't show the meter. Let me test now.\n\nWrite 0x066F=32, step, read heart row."}
